        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Size Dehumidifier Do You Need for a Basement?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size by square footage first, then by how wet the space is. On current pint ratings, a basement that is musty only in summer needs about 35 pints up to 1,500 square feet; one with a damp feel or damp spots needs 50 pints in that range; past 1,500 square feet, damp walls or floors call for a whole-basement unit; and a basement that gets wet after storms needs a drainage fix before any machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the pint rating on the box means</h3>

          <p>
            The number on the box is a daily extraction figure in pints, measured under a standard test, and that test changed in 2019. The Department of Energy now rates capacity at 65 degrees and 60 percent humidity, much closer to real basement conditions than the old 80-degree test. As a result, a unit sold as 50 pints today removes about as much as one sold as 70 pints before the change. When you compare an older listing with a newer one, find out which standard each figure uses.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sizing by square footage and dampness</h3>

          <p>
            Begin with the square footage you want to dry, then adjust for how wet it is. A basement that smells musty only in humid weather needs less capacity than one with damp patches on the walls, and one with standing water after rain needs a drainage fix before any dehumidifier can help. The table below is a practical starting point built on current-standard pint ratings.
          </p>

