          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why sizing up usually pays</h3>

          <p>
            If you are torn between two sizes, choose the bigger one. A larger unit hits the target sooner and then cycles off, while an undersized one runs nonstop and still falls short. The running-cost gap between a 35-pint and a 50-pint machine is small, and the bigger unit usually logs fewer hours of actual running. The exception is a cold room, where a larger compressor unit does nothing about frosting; there, look at a desiccant model or a unit specifically rated for lower temperatures.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Closed-off rooms count as separate spaces</h3>

          <p>
            Layout matters as well. A dehumidifier dries only the air it can reach, so a basement divided into small rooms with closed doors behaves like several separate spaces. Leave the doors open and place the unit centrally, run more than one portable, or move to a ducted whole-basement system that draws from every room.
          </p>
