        <div id="drainage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Drain a Basement Dehumidifier?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Run a garden hose downhill to a floor drain or sump pit if one sits lower than the unit, use a built-in or external pump if the drain is higher, and plan a drain line from day one for a whole-basement unit. Emptying a bucket by hand is the fallback, not the plan, because drainage decides how much drying you actually get.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the bucket costs you drying time</h3>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier halted by a full bucket is not removing any water. A 50-pint unit in a wet space can fill a typical bucket in 12 to 24 hours, and unless someone empties it promptly, humidity climbs back while the machine waits. Across a whole summer, a bucket-only setup tends to deliver far less drying than its rating suggests, purely because of that downtime.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gravity drainage</h3>

          <p>
            Gravity is the simplest answer. Most portables have a hose port: connect a standard garden hose, run it to a floor drain or sump pit, and keep it sloping downhill the whole way. If you need more fall, set the unit on a sturdy stand or a couple of concrete blocks. Make sure the hose cannot kink, and keep its end above any standing water in the pit so the line does not siphon or back up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When you need a pump</h3>

          <p>
            If the closest drain is higher than the unit, such as a laundry sink or a window, you need a pump. A built-in pump like the one in the GE APER50LZ can push water up several feet and across a room through thin tubing. An external condensate pump works with any gravity-drain unit and is often the cheaper route if you already own a good dehumidifier. Either way, add the pump to the things you check every month.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Drain lines for whole-basement units</h3>

          <p>
            Whole-basement units are designed around a drain line from the start. Most installers route them to a floor drain or sump pit, and some include a pump. In a finished basement, plan the route before the machine arrives, because a drain line strung across a carpeted floor is the kind of thing that never gets fixed later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Where Should a Basement Dehumidifier Go, and What Humidity Should You Set?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Put it where the air is wettest, with open space all around it, and set it to 50 percent to start. That usually means the middle of the open area or near the damp wall, at least 6 to 12 inches from walls and furniture so nothing blocks the intake or exhaust. Tucking it into a closet or behind boxes turns a 50-pint machine into a small one. If the basement has a sump pit, placing the unit beside it handles drainage and puts it close to one of the wettest spots.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Close the space off</h3>

          <p>
            Shut the basement windows and the door at the top of the stairs while the unit runs. An open window in humid weather leaves the dehumidifier trying to dry the whole neighborhood. Seal obvious leaks at the rim joist and dryer vent, and confirm the dryer itself vents outdoors; one that vents into the basement adds gallons of water with every load.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dial in the humidity setting</h3>

          <p>
            Begin at 50 percent, and check it with a separate hygrometer instead of trusting the unit, because built-in sensors read the air right at the intake and are often a few points off. Once the space holds steady and the musty smell has gone, try 45 percent if condensation still shows on pipes or windows. Going below 40 rarely adds anything except electricity use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Expect two hard weeks</h3>

          <p>
            The first two weeks are the toughest. A basement that has been damp for months stores moisture in its concrete, wood, stored boxes and drywall, and the unit will run almost continuously while it draws that down. Run time usually falls substantially after that. If it never falls, the basement has an ongoing water source the machine cannot outrun, and grading, gutters or wall sealing need attention.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Much Does a Basement Dehumidifier Cost to Run?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Expect roughly $15 to $40 a month in summer for an Energy Star 50-pint portable that cycles normally in a basement that has already been dried down. A typical unit draws around 400 to 600 watts while the compressor runs. At the U.S. average electricity rate of roughly 16 to 18 cents per kilowatt-hour, nonstop running would cost about $1.60 to $2.60 a day, but a properly sized unit cycles on and off, and the monthly figure shifts with climate and how leaky the space is.
          </p>

