        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dehumidifier does a basement need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most basements up to about 1,500 square feet with ordinary summer dampness need a 50-pint unit on the current DOE test. Small, only mildly musty spaces can use a 35-pint unit. Very large or persistently wet basements, especially those with damp walls, are better served by an 80-pint-class whole-basement machine. If you are caught between two sizes, go bigger rather than smaller.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should a basement dehumidifier be set to?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start at 50 percent and confirm the reading with a separate hygrometer. If the musty smell has gone and no condensation forms on pipes or windows, leave it there. Drop to 45 percent if moisture still shows up. Going under 40 percent wastes electricity without adding meaningful mold protection, since mold struggles to grow below about 50 percent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should a basement dehumidifier run all the time?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It should run whenever humidity sits above the set point, which in a damp basement can mean almost nonstop for the first week or two. Once the space has dried out, a correctly sized unit should cycle on and off. If it still never stops after a couple of weeks, it is undersized, the room is too cold for it, or water is still getting in.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a dehumidifier still work in a cold basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standard compressor units lose much of their capacity below about 60 to 65 degrees because the coil frosts. Desiccant units keep working in much colder air, and whole-basement compressor units built for below-grade spaces handle the 50s far better than portables. If your basement stays cool all year, choose one of those types over a typical big-box portable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is a built-in pump worth paying for?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A pump earns its cost when the drain sits higher than the dehumidifier or too far away for a downhill hose. A floor drain or sump pit beside the unit works fine with a gravity hose. A laundry sink, a window or a drain across the room usually calls for a pump, either built into the unit or added as an external condensate pump.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a dehumidifier get rid of basement mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It helps stop new growth by holding humidity below the level most molds need, but it will not kill mold that is already there or remove stains. Clean visible growth with a suitable product, throw out heavily contaminated porous materials, and then let the dehumidifier keep the space dry. Get professional help for patches larger than about 10 square feet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to run a basement dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A typical Energy Star 50-pint portable draws roughly 400 to 600 watts while running. Cycling normally in a basement that has already dried out, that often comes to around $15 to $40 a month in summer at average U.S. electricity rates. Running nonstop costs more, which is why fixing air leaks and water sources pays back quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

